---
<Exploration title="Technical editorial" active="editorial" theme="editorial">
  <header class="site-nav section-frame"><Wordmark /><nav aria-label="Product navigation"><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a><a href={url('/docs')}>Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/hantswilliams/querysolo">GitHub ↗</a></nav></header>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="editorial-hero section-frame"><div class="edition-line"><span>A LOCAL-FIRST LAKEHOUSE</span><span>DEVELOPER PREVIEW / 2026</span></div><h1>Less infrastructure.<br /><em>More understanding.</em></h1><div class="editorial-intro"><span class="large-asterisk" aria-hidden="true">✳</span><p>DuckDB for the work. Iceberg for the tables.<br />A gauge to tell you what a query might take.<br />QuerySolo puts them in a project on your laptop.</p><div><a class="button" href={url('/docs/install')}>Build your first project ↗</a><p class="small-note">macOS & Linux · From source · Apache 2.0</p></div></div></section>
    <section id="architecture" class="architecture section-frame"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / The architecture</p><h2>A few parts.<br />A clear relationship.</h2></div><p>One local core serves the desktop app and CLI.<br />Here’s what each part is responsible for.</p></div><div class="architecture-sheet"><div class="diagram-title"><span>ON YOUR MACHINE</span><span>EXPAND A SECTION BELOW ↓</span></div><div class="architecture-diagram"><div class="diagram-entry"><span>DESKTOP APP</span><b>+</b><span>COMMAND LINE</span></div><span class="diagram-connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div class="diagram-engine"><div><span>01</span><strong>The gauge</strong><p>Plan + manifests → estimate</p></div><span class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><span>02</span><strong>DuckDB</strong><p>SQL → execution</p></div></div><span class="diagram-connector" aria-hidden="true">↕</span><div class="diagram-storage"><div><span>03 / CATALOG</span><strong>Iceberg REST</strong><small>SQLite catalog state</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">↔</span><div><span>04 / TABLES</span><strong>Parquet + Iceberg</strong><small>Data files + metadata</small></div></div><div class="diagram-bottom"><span>LOCAL WAREHOUSE</span><span>OR ATTACH PARQUET IN S3 →</span></div></div><div class="architecture-details"><details open><summary>01 / Estimate before execution <span>+</span></summary><p>The gauge reads the optimized query plan and table metadata to estimate scan size, peak memory, and time. A Red verdict refuses a run unless you override it.</p><a href={url('/docs/gauge')}>Read the gauge’s limits ↗</a></details><details><summary>02 / Run with DuckDB <span>+</span></summary><p>The local DuckDB engine executes your SQL. Results stream to the app; the same core is available through the CLI and local HTTP API.</p><a href={url('/docs/api')}>Inspect the API ↗</a></details><details><summary>03 / Keep track with an open catalog <span>+</span></summary><p>Catalog state lives in SQLite and is served on a loopback Iceberg REST endpoint. Other engines can connect while the catalog is running.</p><a href={url('/docs/catalog')}>Inspect the catalog ↗</a></details><details><summary>04 / Keep your data in open formats <span>+</span></summary><p>Local imports become Iceberg tables on Parquet. Attaching a remote Parquet prefix registers files in place. Back up both your data and catalog state.</p><a href={url('/docs/tables')}>Understand your tables ↗</a></details></div></div></section>
    <section class="editorial-principles section-frame"><p class="eyebrow">02 / What that means for your work</p><div class="principle"><span>01</span><h3>A query comes with context.</h3><p>See an estimate and its reasoning before the work starts. Compare predictions with completed runs in the Gauge screen.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>02</span><h3>A good question becomes a model.</h3><p>Save SQL as a dbt model with checks. Run the project through the catalog. Keep versions of your definitions.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>03</span><h3>The table has a life beyond the app.</h3><p>DuckDB and pyiceberg are tested against the catalog. Spark and Trino interoperability is covered by the project’s engine smoke tests.</p></div></section>
    <section class="editorial-proof section-frame"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Inspect the evidence</p><h2>A claim should<br />have a next click.</h2></div><div class="evidence-links"><a href={url('/docs/remote')}><span>Public-data walkthrough</span><strong>472 million rows, attached in place.</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href={url('/docs/transactions')}><span>Known constraints</span><strong>What the transaction layer supports.</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href="https://github.com/hantswilliams/querysolo/tree/main/core/tests"><span>Source and tests</span><strong>Read the implementation.</strong><b>↗</b></a></div></section>
    <Availability />
    <section class="closing section-frame"><p class="eyebrow">Start with something you can inspect</p><h2>A folder. A table. A question.</h2><a class="button" href={url('/docs/install')}>Open the quickstart ↗</a></section>
  </main>
</Exploration>
